  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224458/assets/b15000ac7f2de0770140ba289a8f5224433bfb6570d985ab184eacba016c97c4.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rectangle?",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224458/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rectangle is a quadrilateral where all angles are right with two pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. It has no right angles, therefore, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, all its angles are right, so it is a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rectangle since all its angles are right.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite. Since there are no pairs of parallel sides, it is not a rectangle.\n\nTherefore, shapes $Q$ and $R$ are rectangles. The shapes $P$ and $S$ are not rectangles.",
